{
  "comprehensible": "The house was in a state of dignified but shabby decline, because the people who lived there had moved away to mainland Europe a long time ago.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "genteel decay",
      "gloss": "a kind of decline that still keeps a respectable, upper-class appearance"
    },
    {
      "expression": "repaired to",
      "gloss": "went to; moved to (a formal or old-fashioned use)"
    },
    {
      "expression": "the Continent",
      "gloss": "mainland Europe (especially from a British point of view)"
    }
  ],
  "grammar": "The phrase 'its occupants having long since repaired to the Continent' is an absolute construction: it has its own subject ('its occupants') and a participle ('having repaired'), and it gives the reason or accompanying circumstance for the main clause. It is not a separate sentence, and 'having long since' means the moving away happened well before the time being described.",
  "translation": "这栋房子处于一种体面而衰败的状态，因为它的住户早已迁往欧洲大陆。"
}

{
